  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 300 PM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nCurrently-Tonight... KMLB radar imagery shows scattered generally\nnortheast morning showers and lightning storms mainly from Cape \nCanaveral to Lake Okeechobee. GOES-19 satellite imagery and local \nobservations indicate cloudy skies across east central Florida\n(mainly north of the Treasure Coast). Analysis charts show a cold\nfront over the southeastern US, as well as northern Florida which\nextends from the remnants of Hurricane Isiais near the \nKentucky/Virginia border. Temperatures are currently in the mid \n70s to low 80s north of Melbourne to Lake Kissimmee and in the \nmid 80s to low 90s to the south. Dew points are currently in the \nmid 70s to low 80s. \n\nThe aforementioned cold front is expected to move southeast \nacross east central Florida tonight before reaching the Treasure \nCoast into Monday morning. Scattered to numerous showers (50-80% \nchance) and lightning storms are expected along and ahead of the \nfront this afternoon/evening before rain chances diminish by \naround 9PM. Guidance shows MLCAPE up to 1,000-3,000 J/kg, 0-6km \nshear up to 20-30kts, PWATs at 2.3-2.5\" (at the maximum percentile\nfor the date), a 500mb temperature of -4.6C, and a LLJ at 25kts \nacross northern east central Florida. The aforementioned weather \nparameters are sufficient for isolated storms capable of \noccasional to frequent lightning, wind gusts up to 40-50mph, and \nheavy rainfall (1-2+\" in 60 minutes). There is a Marginal Risk \n(level 1 of 5) for excessive rainfall with the potential for minor\nflooding of roadways and low-lying areas. Expect cooler low \ntemperatures behind the front with lows in the mid 60s to mid 70s \nforecast (coolest north of I-4). \n\nMonday-Tuesday... Drier air is expected to filter in behind the\ncold front into next week with PWAT values below 1\" north of the\nTreasure Coast and between 1.0-2.3\" across the Treasure Coast.\nModest northerly flow is forecast through Tuesday with mostly dry\nweather expected as a longwave ridge builds over the eastern US.\nHowever, isolated showers with embedded lightning cannot be ruled\nover over southern St. Lucie and Martin counties on Monday. Expect\nseasonable high temperatures in the mid to upper 80s with lows in\nthe mid 60s to low 70s. \n\nWednesday-Saturday... Deterministic models and LREF-clusters are \nin fairly good agreement on the weather pattern through mid week \nwith the extent of the apex of the mid/upper ridge expected to \nreach the Ohio Valley. However, deterministic models and LREF- \nclusters continue to diverge in solutions towards the end of the\nforecast period Friday into Saturday which is where uncertainty \nin the forecast increases. Either way, a mid/upper level trough \nis likely to deepen over the eastern CONUS Friday into Saturday\nwith uncertainty in whether it deepens as far south as the \nsoutheastern US. Moisture is expected to increase mid to late week\nas onshore flow develops. Coverage of showers and lightning \nstorms gradually increases through the forecast period with flow \nbecoming northerly on Saturday. Seasonable to slightly above \nnormal highs are forecast with seasonable to above normal low \ntemperatures.",
